#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# subresultant_polynomials

#% text
# `subresultant_polynomials(p, q, x)` gives the subresultant polynomials `[S0, S1, ..., Sk]` of `p` and `q` in `x`, where `k` is the smaller of their degrees.

#% text
# `subresultant_polynomials(p, q, x, modulus: n)` computes them modulo the prime `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `Sj` is the determinant polynomial of the j-th Sylvester submatrix; it has degree at most `j`, and `S0` is the resultant.

#% item
# For `deg p > deg q`, `Sk` is `lc(q)^(deg p - deg q - 1) q`.

#% item
# The last nonzero `Sj` is a greatest common divisor of `p` and `q` up to a factor free of `x`; defective (degree below `j`) and zero subresultants are included.

#% item
# The chain is computed with Lazard's and Ducos's optimisations (Ducos 2000): every division is exact, so the coefficients may contain parameters.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
subresultant_polynomials(x^4 - 2*x^2 + x, x^3 - x - 1, x)
#% output
#= [5, -1 + 3*x, 2*x - x^2, -1 - x + x^3]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A common factor `x - 2` gives zero subresultants below degree 1:

#% cell
subresultant_polynomials((x - 2)*(x^2 + 1), (x - 2)*(x + 5), x)
#% output
#= [0, -52 + 26*x, -10 + 3*x + x^2]

#% text
# Parameters:

#% cell
subresultant_polynomials(x^3 + p*x + r, 3*x^2 + p, x)
#% output
#= [4*p^3 + 27*r^2, 9*r + 6*p*x, p + 3*x^2]

#% text
# Modulo a prime:

#% cell
subresultant_polynomials(x^4 + x + 1, x^3 + 2, x, modulus: 3)
#% output
#= [0, 2 + x, 1 + 2*x, 2 + x^3]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `subresultants`, `subresultant_polynomial_remainders`, `resultant`, `polynomial_gcd`
